WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:15.830
<v Ira Glass>I don't know when I got this idea that a captain's log be pretty interesting to read. Probably it was from watching Star Trek as a kid. You know, they do that whole captain's log star date thing. But in fact, in real life, here on planet earth, most captain's logs--
<v Ira Glass>我不知道什么时候产生了这样的想法：船长日志读起来应该会很有趣。可能是我小时候看《星际迷航》受的影响。你知道，他们总是做那种“船长日志，星历日期”之类的记录。但实际上，在现实生活中，在地球上，大多数船长日志——

00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:24.410
<v Erik Larson>Usually they're incredibly boring. I mean, it's like, you know, it's really primarily meant to be a record of ships' positions, weather.
<v Erik Larson>通常都无聊透顶。我的意思是，这基本上就是为了记录船只的位置和天气。

00:00:24.410 --> 00:00:40.840
<v Ira Glass>That's the story on Erik Larson. He ran into this when he was writing a book about this hurricane that destroyed the city of Galveston in 1900. He thought that ships at sea might have interesting observations about the formation of this historic weather event, this storm. So he looked at lots of ships' logs.
<v Ira Glass>埃里克·拉尔森（Erik Larson）就是这么发现的。他在写一本关于1900年摧毁加尔维斯顿市飓风的书时遇到了这种情况。他原以为海上的船只可能会对这场历史性天气事件——这场风暴的形成有有趣的观察记录。所以他查阅了大量的航海日志。

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:51.390
<v Erik Larson>And I found it very frustrating, very frustrating. It was just, like, routine references. And meanwhile, here's this gigantic storm looming on the horizon.
<v Erik Larson>我发现这非常令人沮丧，真的非常令人沮丧。里面全是些例行公事的记载。而与此同时，一场巨大的风暴正在地平线上酝酿。

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:53.840
<v Ira Glass>And why? What would they way in their logs?
<v Ira Glass>为什么会这样？他们在日志里会写什么呢？

00:00:53.840 --> 00:01:14.250
<v Erik Larson>Nothing. They would just list, you know, some wind speed, wind direction. Sometimes I think that a ship could be passing Krakatoa when it was blowing up, and there would be no reference, except maybe a sudden change in the air temperature.
<v Erik Larson>什么都不写。他们只是列出风速、风向。有时候我觉得，哪怕一艘船经过正在喷发的喀拉喀托火山，日志里也不会有任何提及，顶多可能提到气温的突然变化。

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:28.050
<v Ira Glass>A ship's log does record anything unusual that happens on the ship itself, any damage to the ship. But really, it's about legal and administrative responsibilities. If cargo's damaged, insurers are going to need a record, that kind of thing.
<v Ira Glass>航海日志确实会记录船上发生的任何异常情况，或是船只的任何损坏。但实际上，它主要关乎法律和行政责任。如果货物损坏了，保险公司会需要一份记录，诸如此类。

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:41.060
<v Ira Glass>Which is why this one captain's log that Larson found really stood out. He was researching the sinking of the Lusitania for his book, Dead Wake . And he got his hands on the captain's log for the U-boat that sunk the Lusitania in 1915.
<v Ira Glass>正因如此，拉尔森发现的一份船长日志才显得格外突出。当时他正在为自己的书《死寂》（Dead Wake）研究卢西塔尼亚号的沉没。他搞到了1915年击沉卢西塔尼亚号的那艘U型潜艇的船长日志。

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:59.410
<v Ira Glass>The prose is businesslike and direct, just like most logs, even in translation from the German. But this captain just includes way more stuff, like way more detail than other logs that Larson had read. When Captain Walter Schwieger spots the Lusitania at 2:20 submarine time, he notes four funnels and two masts.
<v Ira Glass>文字简洁干练，就像大多数日志一样，即使是翻译成德语也是如此。但这位船长包含的信息量要多得多，比拉尔森读过的其他日志详细得多。当沃尔特·施威格（Walter Schwieger）船长在潜艇时间2:20发现卢西塔尼亚号时，他记录下了四个烟囱和两根桅杆。

00:01:59.410 --> 00:02:17.800
<v Erik Larson>You know, starboard ahead, coming from south to southwest, it's steered towards Galley Head, which is a Maritime landmark there in the Irish sea. The ship is made out to be a large passenger steamer. Then he says submerged to a depth of 11 meters, and at full speed, took a course converging.
<v Erik Larson>“右舷前方，从南向西南方向驶来，正朝着加利角（Galley Head）航行，”那是爱尔兰海的一处海上地标。“该船看来是一艘大型客轮。”然后他说，“下潜至11米深度，全速前进，取汇合航向。”

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:20.060
<v Ira Glass>So, so far, very matter of fact.
<v Ira Glass>到目前为止，非常客观冷静。

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:39.160
<v Erik Larson>Very matter of fact. Now, what's important to know here as well is that, meanwhile, what's happening aboard his submarine is total chaos. He's doing a fast dive, which, depending on conditions, sometimes involved having members of the crew race to the bow, run to the front of the submarine to add ballast.
<v Erik Larson>非常冷静。现在，这里同样重要的一点是，与此同时，他的潜艇里正处于完全的混乱之中。他正在进行紧急下潜，根据当时的情况，有时需要船员们冲向船头，跑到潜艇前端增加配重。

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:40.760
<v Ira Glass>To add that extra weight.
<v Ira Glass>增加额外的重量。

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:47.300
<v Erik Larson>Yes. So he's doing this very fast dive. There's chaos aboard the submarine. There's a lot of energy, a lot of excitement.
<v Erik Larson>是的。所以他正在进行这种快速下潜。潜艇内一片混乱，充满紧张感和躁动。

00:02:47.300 --> 00:03:11.740
<v Ira Glass>That human drama doesn't make it into the log, even this one. 2:35 PM submarine time, the ship that he's chasing turns starboard towards a town called Queenstown. He runs at the ship at high speed, which Larson says was actually not very fast at all. 3:10 PM submarine time, the log says he takes a clean bow shot at a distance of 700 meters. He names the kind of torpedo he uses and the depth adjustment he makes, the angle.
<v Ira Glass>那种人性化的戏剧冲突并没有被写进日志，即使是这一份也没有。潜艇时间下午2:35，他正在追击的船转向右舷，驶向一个叫昆斯敦（Queenstown）的城镇。他以高速冲向那艘船，拉尔森说这其实一点也不快。潜艇时间下午3:10，日志上写着他在700米的距离上进行了一次干净利落的船头射击。他写明了他使用的鱼雷种类、深度调整，以及角度。

00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:29.970
<v Erik Larson>"Torpedo hits starboard side right behind the bridge." There it is. That's the actual firing of the torpedo in the attack. Then, though, he starts in with a fairly elaborate-- at least for a German submarine commander-- narrative.
<v Erik Larson>“鱼雷击中右舷，就在舰桥后方。”就是这样。这就是攻击中鱼雷实际发射的时刻。不过，随后他开始写下一段相当详尽的——至少对于一名德国潜艇指挥官来说——叙述。

00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:50.470
<v Erik Larson>He's watching this now through his periscope. "An usually strong explosion or detonation takes place with a very strong explosive cloud. The cloud reaches far beyond the front funnel. The explosion of the torpedo must have been accompanied by a second one," parentheses, "boiler or coal or powder?" Question mark.
<v Erik Larson>他现在正通过潜望镜观察这一切。“发生了一次异常强烈的爆炸或引爆，伴有非常浓烈的爆炸烟云。烟云延伸到前烟囱之外。鱼雷的爆炸一定伴随着第二次爆炸，”括号，“锅炉、煤炭还是火药？”问号。

00:03:50.470 --> 00:04:09.200
<v Erik Larson>"The superstructure right above the point of the impact and the bridge are torn asunder. Fire breaks out, and the smoke envelops the high bridge. The ship stops." He's incorrect there, by the way. The ship continued at 18 knots because its engines were knocked out, and you need engines in order to stop a ship.
<v Erik Larson>“冲击点正上方的上层建筑和舰桥被炸得粉碎。火灾爆发，浓烟笼罩了高大的舰桥。船停下了。”顺便说一句，他这里错了。船依然以18节的速度航行，因为它的引擎被摧毁了，而让一艘船停下需要引擎。

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:22.940
<v Erik Larson>"The ship stops immediately and heels over to starboard very quickly." And he says, "immersing simultaneously at the bowel. Great confusion ensues on board. The boats are made clear, and some of them are lowered to the water."
<v Erik Larson>“船立即停止，并迅速向右舷倾斜。”他还说，“同时船头下沉。船上一片大乱。救生艇被清理出来，其中一些被放下水面。”

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:24.560
<v Ira Glass>He means the lifeboats there.
<v Ira Glass>他指的是那里的救生艇。

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:35.750
<v Erik Larson>Yes, the lifeboats. "In doing so, some must have lost their heads. Some boats, full to the capacity, are lowered, touch the water with either stem or stern first, and founder immediately."
<v Erik Larson>是的，救生艇。“在放下过程中，有些人一定乱了阵脚。一些满载的救生艇被放下去时，船头或船尾先触水，随即倾覆。”

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:50.970
<v Ira Glass>This is actually happening as they're filling with water, capsizing, sinking. It is a grisly scene-- people throwing themselves off the ship into the water and getting sucked underneath the sinking vessel, people getting crushed by the lifeboats. 1,198 died.
<v Ira Glass>这实际上是发生在它们进水、倾覆、下沉的过程中。这是一幕可怕的场景——人们从船上跳入水中，被卷入下沉船只的下方，有人被救生艇挤压。1198人死亡。

00:04:50.970 --> 00:05:00.240
<v Ira Glass>Captain Schwieger watches through his periscope, and he can't actually hear anything. What he's seeing is like this terrible silent movie. 3:25 PM submarine time in the log.
<v Ira Glass>施威格船长通过潜望镜观察，他实际上听不到任何声音。他所看到的就像是一部可怕的无声电影。日志时间为潜艇时间下午3:25。

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:11.920
<v Erik Larson>"As it seems as if the steamer will keep above water only a short time, we dived to a depth of 24 meters and ran out to sea." Now, what he means there is he doesn't have to hang around and fire another torpedo.
<v Erik Larson>“由于这艘轮船看起来只能在水面上停留很短时间，我们下潜到24米的深度并驶向公海。”他这里的意思是，他不必逗留再去发射第二枚鱼雷了。

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:23.640
<v Erik Larson>However, here he says the most controversial statement in is log. "It would have been impossible for me anyhow to fire a second torpedo into this crushing crowd of humanity trying to save their lives."
<v Erik Larson>然而，他在日志里说了最有争议的一句话。“无论如何，我都不可能向这群拼命挣扎的挤作一团的人群发射第二枚鱼雷。”

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:43.400
<v Ira Glass>"It would have been impossible for me to fire a second torpedo into this crushing crowd of humanity trying to save their lives." This sentence is controversial because he's never like this anywhere else in the log, never sympathetic to his enemies this way. He never expresses feelings like this in this log that's going to be read, you know, by his superior officers. Why say this?
<v Ira Glass>“我不可能向这群拼命挣扎的挤作一团的人群发射第二枚鱼雷。”这句话之所以有争议，是因为他在日志的其他任何地方都没有这样过，从未对他的敌人表现出这种同情。在这份即将被他的上级军官阅读的日志中，他从未表达过这样的感受。为什么要这么说？

00:05:43.400 --> 00:06:13.430
<v Erik Larson>I don't know what to think. I would like to think that this captain had that level of remorse, right, and that he acknowledged it right there at the time. I don't think so. My money is on a post-patrol revision that he may have added that after the fact, after everybody recognized how much the world had been repulsed by this attack on men, women, and children on an unarmed passenger ship.
<v Erik Larson>我不知道该怎么想。我愿意相信这位船长当时确实有这种悔意，并且他在那时就承认了。但我认为并非如此。我敢打赌这是巡逻后的修订，他可能是事后才加上去的，在全世界都对这次针对无武装客船上男女老幼的袭击感到反感之后。

00:06:13.430 --> 00:06:26.220
<v Ira Glass>But if you think about it, even this, it doesn't totally add up. Like, OK, sure, the rest of the world was horrified by the sinking of the Lusitania . But Schwieger lived in Germany, where this was celebrated, a huge victory.
<v Ira Glass>但如果你仔细想想，即使是这样，也完全说不通。比如，好吧，当然，全世界都被卢西塔尼亚号的沉没震惊了。但施威格生活在德国，在那里这是一场备受赞誉的巨大胜利。

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:40.320
<v Ira Glass>Did he want to change the log because he thought like, OK, maybe they would actually lose the war and he worried about how this was going to look? Did he just not want this to be his legacy, how he'd be talked about 100 years later as, you know, I'm doing right this second?
<v Ira Glass>他想修改日志是因为他觉得，好吧，也许他们真的会输掉战争，而他担心这看起来会怎样？他只是不想让这成为他的遗产，不想让人们在100年后像我现在这样讨论他吗？

00:06:40.320 --> 00:07:01.630
<v Ira Glass>Well, today on our program, we bring you captain's logs of various sorts. For every story in today's show, we found a terse, businesslike record of some kind of some event in the past. Some of these are historic events. Some of them are small and personal. And what we're going to do is we're going to go back and reconstruct what really happened, which is way more complicated and emotional than the logs.
<v Ira Glass>今天在我们的节目中，我们将为您带来各种各样的船长日志。对于今天节目中的每一个故事，我们都找到了一种简练的、公事公办的记录，记录了过去发生的某种事件。其中一些是历史事件，一些是微小而私人的。我们将要做的是，回去重建真正发生的事情，那比日志要复杂和感人得多。

00:07:01.630 --> 00:07:19.610
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us, mateys.
<v Ira Glass>来自芝加哥WBEZ，这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。和我们待在一起吧，伙伴们。

00:07:19.610 --> 00:07:40.620
<v Ira Glass>Act 1, "Cookies and Monsters." So part of what's interesting about reading captain's logs is then going back and piecing together the true story that they're only sort of telling. And that is what PJ Vogt does in this next story. He starts picking something apart and asking questions, and he ends up somewhere very different from where he starts. Here's PJ.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，“饼干与怪兽”。阅读船长日志的有趣之处在于回去拼凑出他们仅仅只是粗略叙述的真实故事。这也是PJ·沃格特（PJ Vogt）在接下来的故事中所做的。他开始剖析某件事并提出问题，最终他到达了一个与起点截然不同的地方。这是PJ的故事。

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:56.940
<v Pj Vogt>I've got a friend, let's call him Dale. Dale has a Gmail address that's pretty generic, like [email protected] . And people with email addresses like these get a lot of emails that just aren't meant for them, like email wrong numbers. And this happens to him all the time.
<v Pj Vogt>我有个朋友，咱们叫他戴尔。戴尔有一个非常大众化的Gmail地址，比如[email protected]。拥有这类电子邮件地址的人会收到很多根本不是发给他们的邮件，就像电子邮件里的“打错电话”。这对他来说是常有的事。

00:07:56.940 --> 00:08:12.150
<v Pj Vogt>Last time I saw him, he'd just gotten an email written completely in Spanish from a kid somewhere asking if he could turn in some work late. Dale's a nice guy, but he likes to play pranks. He likes to mess with people. So he answers the emails. Here's one he got a while back.
<v Pj Vogt>上次见到他时，他刚收到一封完全用西班牙语写的邮件，来自某处的孩子问他是否可以迟交作业。戴尔是个好人，但他喜欢搞恶作剧。他喜欢捉弄人。所以他回复了这些邮件。这是他前阵子收到的其中一封。

00:08:12.150 --> 00:08:32.340
<v Dale>I think it started off, "Hey, ladies. To all Calgary area district commissioners and district cookie advisers." And then it started talking about how they had a bunch of stale cookies that they didn't know what to do with, and we've got to move them off the shelves. And if they're past the expiration date, then we can't use them in the next cookie campaign.
<v Dale>我想开头是这样的：“嘿，女士们。致卡尔加里地区的所有区专员和饼干顾问。”然后开始讨论他们有一堆陈饼干不知道该怎么办，我们必须把它们从货架上清理掉。如果它们过了保质期，那我们就不能在下一次饼干活动中使用它们了。

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:48.000
<v Pj Vogt>The emails continue. And Dale learns that the world of professional cookie advising, it's surprisingly bureaucratic. At the top, there's a national cookie adviser. But beneath her, there are provincial cookie advisers who report up. And then beneath them, there are district cookie advisers.
<v Pj Vogt>邮件还在继续。戴尔了解到，专业饼干顾问的世界出奇地官僚化。最上面有全国饼干顾问。在她下面，有向她汇报的省级饼干顾问。再下面，还有区饼干顾问。

00:08:48.000 --> 00:09:06.170
<v Pj Vogt>Dale has no idea what's going on here. But he decides the best thing he could do would be to reply with an intentionally stupid email, detailing a bunch of asinine solutions to the stale cookie problem. He suggests the advisers Sharpie over the expiration dates on the packages. He suggests they eat all the stale cookies themselves.
<v Pj Vogt>戴尔根本不知道这里发生了什么。但他决定自己能做的最好的事情就是用一封故意装傻的邮件回复，详细列出了一堆针对陈饼干问题的愚蠢解决方案。他建议顾问们用记号笔盖掉包装上的保质期。他建议他们自己吃掉所有陈饼干。

00:09:06.170 --> 00:09:15.080
<v Dale>In my mind, I was thinking, no one's going to believe this. What a stupid email to write to somebody. Who would hire a person with suggestions like these?
<v Dale>我在心里想，没人会相信这个。写这种邮件给别人太蠢了。谁会雇佣一个提出这种建议的人呢？

00:09:15.080 --> 00:09:34.600
<v Pj Vogt>Cynthia, who's the Calgary area cookie adviser, responds to Dale's email with complete polite cheerfulness. She sends him a cookie freshness calculator to help him sort his stale cookies from his fresh cookies. So Dale responds with even stupider messages. He was trying to make it more obvious that he was just kidding.
<v Pj Vogt>辛西娅（Cynthia），也就是卡尔加里地区的饼干顾问，以完全礼貌和愉快的态度回复了戴尔的邮件。她发给他一个饼干新鲜度计算器，帮他把陈饼干和新鲜饼干分开。所以戴尔回复了更愚蠢的信息。他试图让对方更明显地看出他在开玩笑。

00:09:34.600 --> 00:09:50.370
<v Dale>I said, "What's the status on the cookies? Yarr. Me so hungry," with a picture of Cookie Monster. And I think she responded something along lines of, "Those orders were supposed to go in a month ago. Or did I misunderstand your question?"
<v Dale>我说：“饼干的情况怎么样了？呀。我好饿，”并附上了一张饼干怪兽的照片。我想她回复的大意是：“那些订单应该一个月前就发出的。还是我误解了你的问题？”

00:09:50.370 --> 00:09:58.590
<v Pj Vogt>Rather than clarifying, Dale asks her, why are we even in the cookie advising business? He says, his clients, they're all about chocolate bars now.
<v Pj Vogt>戴尔没有澄清，而是问她，我们为什么要从事饼干咨询业务？他说，他的客户现在只对巧克力棒感兴趣。

00:09:58.590 --> 00:10:11.430
<v Dale>And Cynthia responded, "Chocolate bars????" Question mark, question mark, question mark, question mark. All of my other suggestions were met with, like, oh, maybe I misunderstood or something. But this one was very emphatic. It was like, "Chocolate bars?"
<v Dale>辛西娅回复说：“巧克力棒？？？？”四个问号。我之前所有的建议都得到了诸如“哦，也许我误解了”之类的回应。但这一条非常坚定。就像是，“巧克力棒？”

00:10:11.430 --> 00:10:19.890
<v Pj Vogt>It actually seemed like Dale had maybe touched a nerve. Because after that, cookie advisor world went completely quiet. Dale was bothered by this.
<v Pj Vogt>这看起来戴尔可能确实触到了痛处。因为那之后，饼干顾问的世界完全安静了。戴尔对此感到困扰。

00:10:19.890 --> 00:10:26.280
<v Pj Vogt>The original email he'd gotten had been meant for a woman named Debbie. What if he had gotten Debbie in trouble, or even just made her look bad?
<v Pj Vogt>他收到的最初那封邮件是发给一个叫黛比（Debbie）的女士的。如果他害黛比惹上了麻烦，或者甚至只是让她看起来很糟糕怎么办？

00:10:26.280 --> 00:10:37.020
<v Dale>I'm a little afraid. I'd like to think that, oh, they just got it sorted out, and now it's funny, and Debbie is in on the joke-- just people trying to do their cookie job.
<v Dale>我有点害怕。我愿意认为，哦，她们只是把它解决了，现在这很有趣，而且黛比也参与了这个玩笑——这只是人们试图做好他们的饼干工作而已。

00:10:37.020 --> 00:10:43.770
<v Pj Vogt>I reached out to the woman who'd been trying to email Debbie, Cynthia, because I wanted to find out if Dale's prank had hurt anybody.
<v Pj Vogt>我联系了那个试图给黛比发邮件的女士辛西娅，因为我想找出戴尔的恶作剧是否伤害了任何人。

00:10:43.770 --> 00:10:45.130
<v Pj Vogt>Hello?
<v Pj Vogt>喂？

00:10:45.130 --> 00:10:46.270
<v Cynthia>Hello.
<v Cynthia>喂。

00:10:46.270 --> 00:10:47.340
<v Pj Vogt>Hi, Cynthia?
<v Pj Vogt>你好，是辛西娅吗？

00:10:47.340 --> 00:10:49.110
<v Cynthia>Yes, it's me.
<v Cynthia>是的，是我。

00:10:49.110 --> 00:10:50.060
<v Pj Vogt>Hi, it's PJ.
<v Pj Vogt>你好，我是PJ。

00:10:50.060 --> 00:10:57.260
<v Ira Glass>Cynthia has multiple sclerosis, and it can be hard for her to talk. So her friend, Sheila, volunteered to help out. I read them the emails.
<v Ira Glass>辛西娅患有多发性硬化症，说话可能有些困难。所以她的朋友希拉（Sheila）自愿帮忙。我给她们读了那些邮件。

00:10:57.260 --> 00:11:10.330
<v Pj Vogt>"And of course, the obvious solution is to eat them during our next member meeting. Please discuss with the rest of area, and I will forward your decision on to national. Thanks so much." Do you remember getting that?
<v Pj Vogt>“当然，显而易见的解决办法是在我们下次会员会议时把它们吃掉。请与区域内其他人讨论，我会把你们的决定转达给全国。非常感谢。”你记得收到过这个吗？

00:11:10.330 --> 00:11:14.740
<v Cynthia>You know, I don't, but--
<v Cynthia>你知道，我不记得了，但是——

00:11:14.740 --> 00:11:28.050
<v Sheila>We get a lot of questions all across Alberta at Cookie Time. Often, they have suggestions that don't always fly. So we find a way to respond do them as best we can.
<v Sheila>我们在阿尔伯塔省的“饼干时间”会收到各种各样的问题。通常，他们提出的建议并不总是行得通。所以我们会尽我们所能找到回复他们的方式。

00:11:28.050 --> 00:11:35.240
<v Ira Glass>Cynthia and Sheila explained that they were part of Girl Guides. In the US, we have Girl Scouts. Most everywhere else in the world, they call them Girl Guides.
<v Ira Glass>辛西娅和希拉解释说她们是女童军（Girl Guides）的一员。在美国，我们有女童子军（Girl Scouts）。在世界上其他大多数地方，她们被称为女童军。

00:11:35.240 --> 00:11:48.630
<v Pj Vogt>If you know anything about Girl Scouts, you won't be too surprised by this. But Cynthia and Sheila have a way of talking that is just unrelenting positive. It's just the Girl Guide way. That's why Cynthia was so patient with Dale. It was her job.
<v Pj Vogt>如果你了解女童子军，你就不会对此感到太惊讶。但辛西娅和希拉有一种说话方式，那种持续不断的积极态度。这就是女童军的方式。这就是为什么辛西娅对戴尔如此耐心。这是她的工作。

00:11:48.630 --> 00:11:58.320
<v Pj Vogt>But she was also very patient with me, even as-- for reasons that I don't quite understand-- I found myself explaining to her the entire pattern of events that had led Dale to email her.
<v Pj Vogt>但她对我也非常耐心，即使——因为一些我不完全理解的原因——我发现自己在向她解释导致戴尔给她发邮件的整个事件过程。

00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:02.600
<v Pj Vogt>I guess the email was meant for Debbie, but it went to a Dale.
<v Pj Vogt>我猜那封邮件是发给黛比的，但发到了戴尔那儿。

00:12:02.600 --> 00:12:07.670
<v Cynthia>Oh. Now it's starting to make a little bit of sense.
<v Cynthia>哦。现在开始有点明白了。

00:12:07.670 --> 00:12:22.360
<v Pj Vogt>This helpful sunny tone, this Girl Guide voice, is hardwired into their original mission statement, which reads, quote, "A guide smiles and sings under all difficulties," end quote. Smiling and singing under all difficulties.
<v Pj Vogt>这种乐于助人的阳光语调，这种女童军式的声音，被深植在她们最初的使命宣言中，上面写道，引用：“向导在任何困难下都会微笑并歌唱”，结束引用。在任何困难下微笑并歌唱。

00:12:22.360 --> 00:12:39.330
<v Pj Vogt>This sounds ridiculous to me, or at least it did, until I heard a truly crazy story about this exact thing, Girl Guides smiling and singing through incredibly extreme circumstances. I first heard about it from a woman named Janie Hampton. She's kind of a Girl Guide expert. She wrote a book about them.
<v Pj Vogt>这对我来说听起来很荒谬，或者至少在听到一个关于此事的真正疯狂的故事之前是这样的，女童军在极其极端的情况下微笑并歌唱。我第一次从一位叫珍妮·汉普顿（Janie Hampton）的女士那里听说这件事。她算是女童军方面的专家。她写了一本关于她们的书。

00:12:39.330 --> 00:12:47.040
<v Janie Hampton>Now I have to admit, when I started writing the book, I thought, you know, I'm going to make this a bit of a satire and laugh at them.
<v Janie Hampton>现在我必须承认，当我开始写这本书时，我想，你知道，我要把它写成某种讽刺作品，嘲笑她们。

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:53.300
<v Pj Vogt>She says laughing at Girl Guides is pretty easy, because most people think of them as not particularly cool, too sincere.
<v Pj Vogt>她说嘲笑女童军很容易，因为大多数人认为她们不是特别酷，太真诚了。

00:12:53.300 --> 00:12:55.110
<v Janie Hampton>What we call "naff" nowadays.
<v Janie Hampton>也就是我们今天所说的“老土”（naff）。

00:12:55.110 --> 00:12:58.510
<v Pj Vogt>What's naff?
<v Pj Vogt>什么是“老土”？

00:12:58.510 --> 00:13:01.180
<v Janie Hampton>Sort of unfashionable, nerdy.
<v Janie Hampton>有点过时，书呆子气。

00:13:01.180 --> 00:13:12.280
<v Pj Vogt>So Janie sets out to tease some nerds. But then she starts researching. And one day, she's deep in the Girl Guides' archive in their London headquarters, and she finds this old notebook.
<v Pj Vogt>所以珍妮开始想要取笑一些书呆子。但随后她开始做研究。有一天，她在伦敦总部的女童军档案馆深处，发现了一本旧笔记本。

00:13:12.280 --> 00:13:19.280
<v Pj Vogt>It's small, 7" by 10". And the book's a handwritten log of nearly everything one Girl Guide troop did years ago.
<v Pj Vogt>它很小，7英寸乘10英寸。这本书是几年前一个女童军小队几乎所做一切的手写日志。

00:13:19.280 --> 00:13:52.430
<v Janie Hampton>And it said, we did skipping, and we did knots, and we did all sorts of jolly things. And then I came across this song that they'd written. And it said, "We sang our song yesterday. And it went, 'We might have been shipped to Timbuktu. We might have been shipped to Kalamazoo. It's not repatriation, nor is it yet starvation. It's simply concentration in Chefoo." And I thought, what on earth does that mean? Concentration in Chefoo?
<v Janie Hampton>上面写着，我们跳绳，我们打绳结，我们做了各种各样快乐的事情。然后我偶然发现了她们写的一首歌。上面写着：“我们昨天唱了我们的歌。歌词是，‘我们可能被送到了廷巴克图。我们可能被送到了卡拉马祖。这不是遣返，也不是饥荒。这仅仅是在芝罘的集中营。’”我想，这到底是什么意思？在芝罘集中营？

00:13:52.430 --> 00:14:04.220
<v Pj Vogt>Janie does not know where Chefoo is. But she's sure it's not in England, so she looks it up. Chefoo is-- was-- a place in China, a coastal city. It's a good 7,000 miles from London.
<v Pj Vogt>珍妮不知道芝罘在哪里。但她确定那不在英格兰，所以她查了一下。芝罘是——曾经是——中国的一个地方，一个沿海城市。距离伦敦足足7000英里。

00:14:04.220 --> 00:14:24.250
<v Pj Vogt>According to the guide's logbook, the song had been written and performed by a group of Girl Guides for a concert on Christmas day, 1942. The Christmas concert, Janie discovers, was held in Chefoo, but not at a school. The Girl Guides sang their song in a concentration camp. Janie was baffled.
<v Pj Vogt>根据那本向导日志，这首歌是由一群女童军在1942年圣诞节的音乐会上创作并演唱的。珍妮发现，圣诞音乐会是在芝罘举行的，但不是在学校。女童军是在集中营里唱她们的歌。珍妮感到困惑。

00:14:24.250 --> 00:14:38.430
<v Pj Vogt>Why would a concentration camp in China have a singing Girl Guide troop? So Janie starts digging. And she finds someone who was there, an actual witness, born in China, who ended up in the same camp as these Girl Guides, this Belgian guy.
<v Pj Vogt>为什么中国的集中营会有唱着歌的女童军小队？所以珍妮开始挖掘。她找到了一个当时在那里的人，一个真正的见证人，出生在中国，最终和这些女童军在同一个营地，是一个比利时人。

00:14:38.430 --> 00:14:42.930
<v Leopold Pander>Leopold Pander, 74 years old.
<v Leopold Pander>利奥波德·潘德（Leopold Pander），74岁。

00:14:42.930 --> 00:14:47.390
<v Pj Vogt>The problem is, it turns out he has absolutely no memory of his time there.
<v Pj Vogt>问题是，事实证明，他对自己在那里的时光完全没有记忆。

00:14:47.390 --> 00:14:52.010
<v Leopold Pander>I tried to remember something, but nothing comes back to me.
<v Leopold Pander>我试图想起点什么，但什么也想不起来。

00:14:52.010 --> 00:15:01.160
<v Pj Vogt>Nothing, except for this nightmare he used to have when he was a kid. At the time, it hadn't made sense to him. But later, he thought it must have taken place at the camp.
<v Pj Vogt>什么也没有，除了他小时候常做的那个噩梦。当时那对他来说毫无意义。但后来，他认为那一定是在集中营里发生的。

00:15:01.160 --> 00:15:04.170
<v Pj Vogt>What was the dream that you would have?
<v Pj Vogt>你做的那个梦是什么样的？

00:15:04.170 --> 00:15:21.530
<v Leopold Pander>Well, I'm there in the hot sun, the blue sky. It's a brown slope. It's a browned earth. And there are big stones next to myself, dirty earth and people running all over the place.
<v Leopold Pander>嗯，我在烈日下，蓝天。这是一个棕色的斜坡。是褐色的土地。我身边有大石头，脏土，人们四处奔跑。

00:15:21.530 --> 00:15:23.050
<v Pj Vogt>Are there sounds?
<v Pj Vogt>有声音吗？

00:15:23.050 --> 00:15:45.380
<v Leopold Pander>No sound, absolutely no sound. Somebody picks me up, and then I wake up. That's all I remember. That dream came back very often.
<v Leopold Pander>没有声音，绝对没有声音。有人把我抱起来，然后我就醒了。这就是我记得的一切。那个梦经常回来。

00:15:45.380 --> 00:15:57.610
<v Pj Vogt>Leopold grows up. And as an adult, he wants to know about this place that he used to dream about, and so he builds a website. He invites people to write in with memories of the camp. And the story he learns is pretty crazy.
<v Pj Vogt>利奥波德长大了。作为一个成年人，他想了解他过去常梦见的那个地方，所以他建立了一个网站。他邀请人们写下他们对集中营的记忆。他了解到的故事非常疯狂。

00:15:57.610 --> 00:16:02.150
<v Reporter>Japan's latest invasion of China, which has already lasted two years, is war on a huge scale.
<v Reporter>日本对中国的最新入侵，已经持续了两年，是一场大规模的战争。

00:16:02.150 --> 00:16:11.690
<v Pj Vogt>So I did not know this, but during World War II, when Japan occupied China, they built concentration camps that were filled with American and British and other European civilians.
<v Pj Vogt>所以我不知道这一点，但在二战期间，当日本占领中国时，他们建立了充满美国、英国和其他欧洲平民的集中营。

00:16:11.690 --> 00:16:13.610
<v Reporter>Japanese put their prisoners of war to work.
<v Reporter>日本人强迫他们的战俘劳动。

00:16:13.610 --> 00:16:18.510
<v Pj Vogt>Civilians who had been living in China. One of those camps was called Weihsien. That was Leopold's camp.
<v Pj Vogt>当时住在中国境内的平民。其中一个集中营叫潍县。那是利奥波德所在的营地。

00:16:18.510 --> 00:16:33.330
<v Pj Vogt>And among the inmates at Weihsien were a group of children. They were American and British. They were mostly the kids of missionaries. And they'd been studying at a boarding school called Chefoo. Japanese troops invaded Chefoo and captured the kids and eventually brought them to Weihsien.
<v Pj Vogt>在潍县的囚犯中有一群孩子。他们是美国人和英国人。他们大多是传教士的孩子。他们曾在芝罘的一所寄宿学校学习。日军入侵芝罘，俘虏了这些孩子，最终把他们带到了潍县。

00:16:33.330 --> 00:16:49.910
<v Janie Hampton>With their teachers, but no parents. So about 150 children who, for four years, were in this camp, and the teachers had very sensibly taken with them books, paper, musical instruments.
<v Janie Hampton>和老师在一起，但没有父母。所以大约有150名孩子在营地里待了四年，老师们非常有远见地带上了书籍、纸张和乐器。

00:16:49.910 --> 00:16:51.810
<v Pj Vogt>And of course, one more thing.
<v Pj Vogt>当然，还有一件事。

00:16:51.810 --> 00:16:59.500
<v Janie Hampton>Brownie uniforms, guide uniforms, all the things they thought, we're going to need this sort of thing to keep the kids occupied.
<v Janie Hampton>布朗尼制服，向导制服，所有他们认为我们可能会需要这些东西来让孩子们保持忙碌的东西。

00:16:59.500 --> 00:17:14.540
<v Pj Vogt>In the Japanese camps, there was very little food. Prisoners died of starvation. In Weihsien, imprisoned monks would smuggle in eggs, and then everybody would share them. And then they'd also have the kids eat the ground up egg shells just to get some extra calcium.
<v Pj Vogt>在日本人的集中营里，食物极少。囚犯死于饥饿。在潍县，被关押的僧侣会偷运鸡蛋进来，然后大家分享。他们还会让孩子们吃磨碎的蛋壳，只是为了获取额外的钙。

00:17:14.540 --> 00:17:31.310
<v Pj Vogt>The log book Janie had found was the record kept by one of the Girl Guides' leaders. The leaders were called Brown Owls. This one was a woman in her 20s. And the tone of her writing was the exact same, cheerful, impervious to bad news tone that Dale's cookie adviser email thread had had.
<v Pj Vogt>珍妮发现的那本日志是其中一位女童军领袖保存的记录。领袖们被称为“猫头鹰”。这一位是一位20多岁的女性。她写作的语调和戴尔饼干顾问邮件串中那种快乐、对坏消息无动于衷的语调一模一样。

00:17:31.310 --> 00:17:45.300
<v Pj Vogt>This is the entry from the day they were marched into the camp. "Hello. What's this? Behind bars. Yes. It's Weihsien camp. Well, I guess there's a good deal of fun to be got out of this. Just the place to earn some badges."
<v Pj Vogt>这是她们被强行带进营地那一天的记录。“你好。这是什么？铁窗后面。是的。这是潍县集中营。好吧，我想这里能找到不少乐趣。正好可以赚几个勋章。”

00:17:45.300 --> 00:17:51.620
<v Pj Vogt>According to the logbook, the Brown Owl ran the troop as if it were any other Girl Guide unit, concentration camp or not.
<v Pj Vogt>根据日志，猫头鹰把小队管理得就像任何其他女童军单位一样，不管是不是在集中营。

00:17:51.620 --> 00:18:07.500
<v Janie Hampton>They were all told it doesn't matter how disgusting the food is, we still want good table manners. It doesn't matter how hungry you are, you're not going to steal. You're still going to do a good deed every day and help other people.
<v Janie Hampton>她们被告知，无论食物多么恶心，我们仍然需要良好的餐桌礼仪。无论你有多饿，你都不能偷窃。你仍然要做每天做一件好事，帮助他人。

00:18:07.500 --> 00:18:19.970
<v Pj Vogt>Obviously the grim sadness of life in a concentration camp should have overpowered this miniature world that the Brown Owls were trying to build for their young girls. But according to Janie, that's not what happened.
<v Pj Vogt>显然，集中营生活的严峻悲伤本应压倒猫头鹰试图为她们的年轻女孩建立的这个微型世界。但根据珍妮的说法，事情并没有那样发生。

00:18:19.970 --> 00:18:26.380
<v Pj Vogt>Instead, it was the Girl Guides who started to exert an influence on the adults around them. They lead by example.
<v Pj Vogt>相反，正是女童军开始对周围的成年人产生影响。她们以身作则。

00:18:26.380 --> 00:18:51.760
<v Janie Hampton>It made a difference to all the adults in this camp and kept them going. The whole atmosphere was better because they had this very strong promise that they wouldn't stop smiling. They wouldn't give up. They would carry on singing songs. They would insist on everybody washing.
<v Janie Hampton>这对营地里的所有成年人产生了影响，并让他们坚持下去。整个气氛变得更好，因为她们有一个非常坚定的承诺，她们不会停止微笑。她们不会放弃。她们会继续唱着歌。她们会坚持让每个人都洗漱。

00:18:51.760 --> 00:19:10.100
<v Pj Vogt>This is the point where I wondered, was this true? I didn't think that anybody was necessarily lying to me. I just thought probably the Brown Owl had left the bad stuff out of her log book. I figured she'd put the best possible spin on an awful situation. That's what Girl Guides do, right?
<v Pj Vogt>这是我感到怀疑的地方，这是真的吗？我不认为有人一定在对我撒谎。我只是觉得猫头鹰可能把坏的事情从她的日志里去掉了。我以为她是在为一个可怕的处境赋予最好的意义。女童军就是这样做的，对吧？

00:19:10.100 --> 00:19:12.410
<v Mary Previte>Oh, the door's open.
<v Mary Previte>哦，门开着。

00:19:12.410 --> 00:19:13.340
<v Phia Bennin>Oh, hello.
<v Phia Bennin>哦，你好。

00:19:13.340 --> 00:19:14.730
<v Mary Previte>Come on in.
<v Mary Previte>进来吧。

00:19:14.730 --> 00:19:22.960
<v Pj Vogt>Fortunately, there's a woman who's still alive and remembers Weihsien. Her name is Mary Previte. She lives in New Jersey. I visit her with my producer, Phia Bennin.
<v Pj Vogt>幸运的是，有一位女性还活着并记得潍县。她的名字叫玛丽·普雷维特（Mary Previte）。她住在新泽西州。我和我的制作人菲亚·贝宁（Phia Bennin）去拜访了她。

00:19:22.960 --> 00:19:29.330
<v Mary Previte>What can I tell you? Oh, by the way, can I pour you some tea? I am so bad about this. I get so busy talking that--
<v Mary Previte>我能告诉你什么呢？哦，对了，能给你们倒杯茶吗？我这方面真是太糟糕了。我谈得太投入以至于——

00:19:29.330 --> 00:19:44.120
<v Pj Vogt>Mary Previte is a small, beautiful 82-year-old woman. She's also one of the happiest people I've ever met. I don't know if anybody I've interviewed has ever fully broken into song unprompted. Mary did-- seven times.
<v Pj Vogt>玛丽·普雷维特是一位娇小、美丽的82岁老太太。她也是我见过的最快乐的人之一。我不知道我采访过的人中是否有人曾主动开口唱歌。玛丽做到了——七次。

00:19:44.120 --> 00:19:51.290
<v Pj Vogt>Also, unlike Leopold, Mary has a phenomenal memory. She told me about the day that Japanese troops arrived at her boarding school.
<v Pj Vogt>而且，与利奥波德不同，玛丽有惊人的记忆力。她告诉了我日军到达她寄宿学校的那一天。

00:19:51.290 --> 00:20:04.150
<v Mary Previte>The day after Pearl Harbor was attacked, the Japanese showed up on the doorstep of our school. They put seals with Japanese writing on everything-- the tables, the chairs, the pianos, the desks. Everything belonged to the Great Emperor of Japan.
<v Mary Previte>珍珠港遇袭的第二天，日本人出现在我们学校的门口。他们在所有东西上都贴上了带有日文的封条——桌子、椅子、钢琴、书桌。一切都属于日本伟大的天皇。

00:20:04.150 --> 00:20:11.570
<v Mary Previte>And then they put arm bands on us. Everyone had to wear an arm band. A for American, B for British, whatever our nationality was.
<v Mary Previte>然后他们给我们戴上了臂章。每个人都必须戴臂章。A代表美国，B代表英国，无论我们的国籍是什么。

00:20:11.570 --> 00:20:25.110
<v Pj Vogt>The girls were eventually transferred into Weihsien. Mary became a concentration camp Girl Guide. This was over 70 years ago. But when Mary talks about the camp, she sounds like she's still there, like she's 12 years old again.
<v Pj Vogt>女孩们最终被转移到了潍县。玛丽成了一名集中营女童军。那已经是70多年前的事了。但当玛丽谈起集中营时，她听起来就像还在那里，就像她又回到了12岁。

00:20:25.110 --> 00:20:30.560
<v Pj Vogt>She said this story about the Brown Owls insisting on good table manners, absolutely true.
<v Pj Vogt>她说关于猫头鹰坚持良好的餐桌礼仪的故事是绝对真实的。

00:20:30.560 --> 00:20:45.180
<v Mary Previte>So you're eating some kind of glop out of a maybe boiled animal grain-- because [CHINESE] is a broomcorn that the Chinese feed to their animals-- was often what they fed us. And you're eating it out of a soap dish or a tin can.
<v Mary Previte>所以你在吃某种粘糊糊的东西，来自某种煮沸的动物谷物——因为高粱是中国的扫帚玉米，中国人喂给动物吃的东西——往往就是他们喂给我们的东西。你用肥皂盒或罐头盒盛着吃。

00:20:45.180 --> 00:21:09.990
<v Mary Previte>And here comes Miss Stark up behind us-- one of our teachers. "Mary Taylor, do not slouch over your food while you are eating. Do not talk while you have food in your mouth. And there are not two sets of manners, one set of manners for the princesses in Buckingham Palace and another set of manners for the Weihsien concentration camp."
<v Mary Previte>然后斯塔克小姐出现在我们身后——我们的老师之一。“玛丽·泰勒，吃东西时不要弯着腰。嘴里有东西时不要说话。礼仪没有两套，白金汉宫公主的一套礼仪，和潍县集中营的另一套礼仪是不一样的。”

00:21:09.990 --> 00:21:22.010
<v Pj Vogt>Mary was separated from her parents, unsure of when she'd BE released, surrounded by pack dogs and men with guns. She says that she spent a lot of her time just thinking about earning merit badges.
<v Pj Vogt>玛丽与父母分离，不知道何时会被释放，被看门狗和持枪的男人包围。她说她大部分时间都在想赚取功勋徽章。

00:21:22.010 --> 00:21:33.110
<v Pj Vogt>In the winter, it would get cold, freezing. But no heat was provided to the prisoners by the guards. Instead, Mary and her friends had to go collect leftover coal shavings from the guards' quarters.
<v Pj Vogt>冬天，天会变得很冷，结冰。但守卫没有给囚犯提供任何取暖设备。相反，玛丽和她的朋友们必须去收集守卫宿舍剩下的煤渣。

00:21:33.110 --> 00:21:38.900
<v Mary Previte>I remember now the ritual of going to Japanese quarters to get the coal dust and carry it back.
<v Mary Previte>我现在还记得去日本营地取煤灰并运回来的仪式。

00:21:38.900 --> 00:21:54.260
<v Pj Vogt>Like making a new pencil from pencil shavings, except the coal is heavy. And it had to be passed bucket by bucket in a line of Girl Guides. Then the shavings were mixed with dust and water and dried in the sun. It was long, hard work.
<v Pj Vogt>就像用铅笔屑制作一支新铅笔，除了煤很重。而且必须一桶接一桶地通过女童军组成的队列传下去。然后煤屑与灰尘和水混合，在太阳下晒干。那是漫长、艰苦的工作。

00:21:54.260 --> 00:22:10.290
<v Pj Vogt>And then at the end of it, you still had to go use that recycled coal in a pot-bellied stove and keep the stove lit so that everybody would be warm. It sounded horrible, like a childhood from a Charles Dickens novel. Except Mary remembers it as being surprisingly fun, a game she could win.
<v Pj Vogt>最后，你还得用那种回收煤，在一个大肚子炉子里把它点燃，并保持炉子一直燃烧，这样大家才会温暖。这听起来很可怕，像查尔斯·狄更斯小说里的童年。但玛丽记得那非常有趣，是一个她能赢的游戏。

00:22:10.290 --> 00:22:47.750
<v Mary Previte>I and my partner, Marjorie Harrison, we won the competition in our dormitory of which stove lighting team made the pot-bellied stove in the winter turn red hot more times than any other girl in the camp. Well, you know, here I am, 82 years old, and what do I choose to tell you? I won. The pot-belly turned red more times with me and Marjorie Harrison than any other girl in our dorm.
<v Mary Previte>我和我的伙伴玛乔丽·哈里森（Marjorie Harrison），我们赢得了宿舍里的比赛，比营地里任何其他女孩更多次地把大肚子炉烧得通红。好吧，你知道，我82岁了，我选择告诉你的竟然是这个？我赢了。我和玛乔丽·哈里森让炉子变红的次数比宿舍里任何其他女孩都多。

00:22:47.750 --> 00:22:55.130
<v Pj Vogt>When you describe it, it sounds like you're describing summer camp instead of describing a concentration camp. Did it feel like summer camp? Did you ever--
<v Pj Vogt>当你描述它时，听起来就像你在描述夏令营，而不是描述集中营。感觉像夏令营吗？你有没有——

00:22:55.130 --> 00:23:30.510
<v Mary Previte>Well, I never was in a summer camp. So I can't give you a-- no, no, no, absolutely not. When you had guard dogs, bayonet drills, electrified wires, barrier walls, pill boxes with armed guards in them, you weren't in a summer camp. Don't misunderstand me. I'm not saying this was Fun City. I'm telling you, we lived a miracle where grownups preserved our childhood.
<v Mary Previte>嗯，我从来没去过夏令营。所以我不能给你一个——不，不，不，绝对没有。当你拥有警犬、刺刀演习、带电铁丝网、障碍墙、里面有武装警卫的碉堡时，你就不是在夏令营。别误会我。我不是说这是“快乐之城”。我是在告诉你，我们经历了一个奇迹，大人们呵护了我们的童年。

00:23:30.510 --> 00:23:45.330
<v Pj Vogt>There's reference in the logbook to the trouble the adults were having keeping it together, but you'd have to know to look for it. A scout leader writes one entry that reads, "Dear me, what a tragedy. Brown Owl had an attack of neuralgia. Let's hope he's better for our meeting.
<v Pj Vogt>日志中有关于成年人们在维持秩序方面遇到困难的记录，但你得知道怎么找。一位童子军领袖写了一条记录，上面写着：“天哪，真是个悲剧。猫头鹰发作了神经痛。希望他在我们的会议前能好起来。”

00:23:45.330 --> 00:23:55.290
<v Pj Vogt>Neuralgia's a nerve disorder. But what that actually meant was that the Brown Owl was having a nervous breakdown. Years later, Mary went and tracked down one of the grownups.
<v Pj Vogt>神经痛是一种神经系统疾病。但那实际上意味着猫头鹰正在精神崩溃。多年后，玛丽去寻找了其中一位大人。

00:23:55.290 --> 00:24:17.950
<v Mary Previte>I said, Miss Carr, what were you feeling when we were in a concentration camp? Well, all the grownups in the camp knew about the rape of Nanking, the atrocities that the soldiers had done when they came to the southern city of Nanking.
<v Mary Previte>我说，卡尔小姐，当我们在集中营时你有什么感觉？好吧，营地里的所有大人们都知道南京大屠杀，士兵们来到南方城市南京时犯下的暴行。

00:24:17.950 --> 00:24:25.280
<v Pj Vogt>Japanese soldiers went door to door, systematically raping and killing tens of thousands of Chinese civilians.
<v Pj Vogt>日本士兵挨家挨户，有系统地强奸和屠杀了数万名中国平民。

00:24:25.280 --> 00:24:54.840
<v Mary Previte>So they knew what could happen. The teachers knew what could happen. So I said to Miss Carr, what were you feeling? She said, well, I would pray to God that when they lined us up along the death trenches-- and they were outside the camp-- when they lined us up to shoot us so our bodies would fall into the death pits, that I would be one of the first so I didn't have to see it.
<v Mary Previte>所以他们知道可能发生什么。老师们知道可能发生什么。所以我对卡尔小姐说，你有什么感觉？她说，好吧，我会向神祈祷，当我们被排成一排站在死亡壕沟边——他们在营地外面——当我们被排成一排等待被枪杀，让我们的身体落入死亡深坑时，我希望我是第一个，这样我就不必看到了。

00:24:54.840 --> 00:25:09.990
<v Pj Vogt>So there were two sets of prayers. At night, the grownups, many of them not much older than the kids themselves, prayed grimly for a fast death. And then they woke up in the morning and they sang songs with the kids, set the bouncy camp melodies.
<v Pj Vogt>所以有两套祈祷。晚上，大人们，其中许多人并不比孩子们大多少，严峻地祈祷能死得快一点。然后他们早上醒来，和孩子们一起唱着歌，设定那些轻快的露营旋律。

00:25:09.990 --> 00:25:38.500
<v Mary Previte>It was like you weren't going to be afraid if you could sing about it. We would sing, (SINGING) Day is done. Gone the sun from the sea, from the hills, from the sky. All is well, safely rest. God is nigh.
<v Mary Previte>就像如果你能唱出来，你就不会害怕。我们会唱，（歌唱）“白昼结束。太阳隐入海中，隐入山后，隐入天空。一切安好，安息吧。神就在身旁。”

00:25:38.500 --> 00:25:55.970
<v Mary Previte>How could you be afraid when you're singing about all is well, safely rest, God is nigh? How could you be afraid of that?
<v Mary Previte>当你唱着一切安好、安息吧、神就在身旁时，你怎么会害怕呢？你怎么会对此感到害怕呢？

00:25:55.970 --> 00:26:35.620
<v Mary Previte>So we were constantly putting things into music. Often, you know, there was a little bit of a twist of fun to it. One of the songs that we sang was, (SINGING) We might have been shipped to Timbuktu. We might have been shipped to Kalamazoo. It's not repatriation or isn't yet stagnation. It's only concentration in Chefoo.
<v Mary Previte>所以我们不断地把事情编进音乐里。通常，你知道，里面会有一点点乐趣的转折。我们唱的其中一首歌是，（歌唱）“我们可能被送到了廷巴克图。我们可能被送到了卡拉马祖。这不是遣返，也不是滞留。这仅仅是在芝罘的集中营。”

00:26:35.620 --> 00:26:53.070
<v Pj Vogt>There probably aren't many places on earth where you have less reason to be cheerful than a concentration camp. But it turns out, in a place like that, being able to be cheerful, to have a positive outlook, it's not dopey or silly. It's how you survive. How you tell the story matters.
<v Pj Vogt>地球上可能没有多少地方比集中营更没有理由快乐。但事实证明，在那样的地方，能够快乐，拥有积极的心态，那并不愚蠢或可笑。那是你生存的方式。你讲述故事的方式很重要。

00:26:53.070 --> 00:27:11.750
<v Mary Previte>For example, one of the things that we sang when the Japanese were marching us into concentration camp was the first verse of Psalm 46. (SINGING) God is our refuge, our refuge and our strength. And on it goes. In trouble, we will not be afraid.
<v Mary Previte>例如，当日本人把我们强行带进集中营时，我们唱的一首歌是诗篇第46篇的第一节。（歌唱）“神是我们的避难所，我们的力量。等等。在危难中，我们不会惧怕。”

00:27:11.750 --> 00:27:33.030
<v Mary Previte>All of these words just sung into our hearts. That sticks. It's like you've got a groove sticking in the gramophone record. I am safe. I am safe. I am safe. That was just profound.
<v Mary Previte>所有这些词都唱进了我们的心里。这会留下来。就像你的唱片机上卡住了一个槽。我是安全的。我是安全的。我是安全的。这真是太深刻了。

00:27:33.030 --> 00:27:42.160
<v Pj Vogt>The first Chefoo brownies warded off despair for four years, until finally, on August 17, 1945, they were rescued.
<v Pj Vogt>第一批芝罘布朗尼队抵挡了四年的绝望，直到最后，在1945年8月17日，她们获救了。

00:27:42.160 --> 00:27:44.230
<v Mary Previte>It was a windy day.
<v Mary Previte>那是一个多风的日子。

00:27:44.230 --> 00:27:47.180
<v Pj Vogt>Mary remembers the American plane flying low over the camp.
<v Pj Vogt>玛丽记得美国飞机在营地上空低空飞行。

00:27:47.180 --> 00:28:00.960
<v Mary Previte>And the parachutes falling from the sky. All I knew was I was running to find whoever it was that was dropping out of the sky beyond the barrier walls.
<v Mary Previte>降落伞从天而降。我所知道的就是我在奔跑，去寻找那些正在障碍墙外从天而降的人。

00:28:00.960 --> 00:28:07.460
<v Leopold Pander>I'm there in the hot sun. It's a blue sky. It's a brown slope. It's a browned earth.
<v Leopold Pander>我在烈日下。这是蓝天。这是棕色的斜坡。是褐色的土地。

00:28:07.460 --> 00:28:10.110
<v Mary Previte>And the people went berserk.
<v Mary Previte>人们都疯了。

00:28:10.110 --> 00:28:12.350
<v Leopold Pander>People running all over the place.
<v Leopold Pander>人们四处奔跑。

00:28:12.350 --> 00:28:15.990
<v Mary Previte>People were crying, screaming, dancing.
<v Mary Previte>人们在哭，在尖叫，在跳舞。

00:28:15.990 --> 00:28:21.250
<v Leopold Pander>Somebody picks me up, and then I wake up.
<v Leopold Pander>有人把我抱起来，然后我就醒了。

00:28:21.250 --> 00:28:34.650
<v Pj Vogt>Leopold says that the nightmare that used to haunt him is just his memory of that day, of being a four-year-old, lost and wandering around a riot of freed concentration camp survivors. Most of the people who are there on liberation day are now dead.
<v Pj Vogt>利奥波德说，那个曾经困扰他的噩梦只是他对那一天的记忆，作为一个四岁的孩子，迷失在获释的集中营幸存者的狂欢中。解放那天在场的大多数人现在都已经去世了。

00:28:34.650 --> 00:28:49.600
<v Pj Vogt>One of the dormitories at Weihsien's a memorial, but mostly this place exists as a footnote in some books on a website designed by a Belgian man and in the memories of the remaining survivors. It's a half disappeared world with a strong pull on the people who do still remember it.
<v Pj Vogt>潍县的宿舍之一是一个纪念馆，但这个地方大多作为一个脚注存在于一些书和比利时男子设计的网站上，以及剩余幸存者的记忆中。这是一个半消失的世界，对那些仍然记得它的人有很强的吸引力。

00:28:49.600 --> 00:29:17.910
<v Pj Vogt>A couple weeks ago at the grocery store, I watched a gang of Brownie Scouts rush down the pet food aisle. They had their uniforms on, covered in merit badges for public speaking and backyard astronomy. They were happy and safe in their own world, a million miles from Weihsien. I wondered if they knew what they might be capable of.
<v Pj Vogt>几周前在杂货店，我看着一群布朗尼女童子军冲进宠物食品通道。她们穿着制服，上面布满了公共演讲和后院天文学的功勋徽章。她们在自己的世界里快乐而安全，距离潍县有百万英里之遥。我不知道她们是否知道自己可能具备什么能力。

00:29:17.910 --> 00:29:29.240
<v Ira Glass>PJ Vogt. He's the co-host of the podcast "Reply All," where this story came from, and which I am a big fan of. You can subscribe to "Reply All" on iTunes, or wherever you get your podcasts.
<v Ira Glass>PJ·沃格特。他是播客“Reply All”的联合主持人，这个故事就来自那里，我非常喜欢那个节目。你可以在iTunes上，或者任何你获取播客的地方订阅“Reply All”。

00:29:29.240 --> 00:29:45.370
<v Ira Glass>On July 28, "Reply All" is going to be appearing in Cast Party, which is a podcast festival broadcast live to movie theaters across the country. It also features "Radiolab" and "Invisibilia." Info and tickets at castparty.org.
<v Ira Glass>7月28日，“Reply All”将出现在Cast Party上，这是一个在全国电影院直播的播客节。它还包括“Radiolab”和“Invisibilia”。信息和门票请访问castparty.org。

00:29:45.370 --> 00:29:58.580
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, Aziz Ansari thinks you don't know how to text. And when he makes the case, I have to say, I think you're going to believe him. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，阿兹·安萨里（Aziz Ansari）认为你不知道该怎么发短信。当他提出这个论点时，我必须说，我想你会相信他的。那是来自芝加哥公共广播电台的节目，稍后继续。

00:29:58.580 --> 00:30:21.220
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose a theme. We bring you different stories on that theme. Today's show, "Captain's Log," stories where we take the unemotional facts in logs and records of various sorts, and we look behind them at the much more emotional, complicated stories that they are so stoically documenting. We've arrived at Act Two of our program, Act Two, "Romancing the Phone."
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周在我们的节目中，当然，我们会选择一个主题。我们会为您带来关于该主题的不同故事。今天的节目，“船长日志”，我们在节目中选取 unemotional（无情感）事实的日志和各种记录，然后看看它们背后那些被极其冷静地记录下来的更加情感化、复杂的故事。我们已经到达了节目的第二幕，第二幕，“浪漫电话”。

00:30:21.220 --> 00:30:37.890
<v Ira Glass>Recently, my wife and I, we were texting some things to each other that were so intense that at the end she suggested we just delete all of our texts that we had ever written to each other ever. And it wasn't like we thought that anybody else was going to see our texts. It's like we just didn't want them to exist in this world any more.
<v Ira Glass>最近，我和妻子之间发了一些非常激烈的短信，最后她建议我们删除我们之间写过的所有短信。这并不是说我们觉得会有其他人看到我们的短信。这只是说我们不希望它们再存在于这个世界上。

00:30:37.890 --> 00:30:49.970
<v Ira Glass>We wanted that history of our conversation to vanish forever from the face of the earth, never think about it again. You know, it's like text messages are this little history. It's like they're our logbook of our interactions with people.
<v Ira Glass>我们希望我们谈话的历史从地球表面永远消失，再也不去想它。你知道，这就像短信是这种小小的历史。就像它们是我们与人互动的日志。

00:30:49.970 --> 00:31:00.580
<v Ira Glass>And of course, usually they're not so emotional. Usually they're pretty terse, which totally makes them perfect for today's program, because their terseness behind it, there can be a whole story.
<v Ira Glass>当然，通常它们并不那么情绪化。通常它们相当简练，这让它们非常适合今天的节目，因为它们的简练背后，可能包含着整个故事。

00:31:00.580 --> 00:31:19.970
<v Ira Glass>And recently, a sociologist started to study this. This was not very traditional sociology. The sociologist Eric Klinenberg at NYU teamed up with a comedian, Aziz Ansari, to examine the texts that people send to each other right when they start dating. And they put out a book with her findings. It's called Modern Romance .
<v Ira Glass>最近，一位社会学家开始研究这个。这不是非常传统的社会学。纽约大学的社会学家埃里克·克林伯格（Eric Klinenberg）与喜剧演员阿兹·安萨里合作，检查人们刚开始约会时互相发送的短信。并出了一本关于她发现的书。叫《现代浪漫》（Modern Romance）。

00:31:19.970 --> 00:31:35.870
<v Ira Glass>And what they did, they spoke to a few hundred people. Their research was conducted in focus groups, like 10 to 20 people in a room, in eight different cities. And then sometimes they would do a version of these focus groups on stage in front of audiences, in New York and Los Angeles.
<v Ira Glass>他们所做的是，他们和几百个人谈过。他们的研究是在焦点小组中进行的，比如一个房间里有10到20个人，在八个不同的城市。有时他们会在纽约和洛杉矶的观众面前进行这些焦点小组的版本。

00:31:35.870 --> 00:31:42.400
<v Ira Glass>What they would do is Aziz would get up, and he would do a little stand up. And then he would introduce Eric, like Eric was the next comic who was coming on stage.
<v Ira Glass>他们会做的是阿兹站起来，做一小段脱口秀。然后他会介绍埃里克，就像埃里克是下一个登台的喜剧演员。

00:31:42.400 --> 00:31:48.280
<v Aziz Ansari>Ladies and gentlemen, please welcome Mr. Eric Klinenberg.
<v Aziz Ansari>女士们，先生们，请欢迎埃里克·克林伯格先生。

00:31:48.280 --> 00:31:59.470
<v Ira Glass>So Eric would come up, and he would talk a little bit about previous work that he'd done. And he would drop this statistic, that in 1950, 23% of American adults were single.
<v Ira Glass>所以埃里克会走上来，谈谈他之前所做的工作。他会抛出这个统计数据，在1950年，23%的美国成年人是单身。

