00:41:54.190 --> 00:42:04.980
<v Aziz Ansari>She wrote back, six minutes later, "Yeah, for sure!" Exclamation. "I'm down. Also, next Thursday is my birthday. So I'm hoping to party it up next weekend." Smiley face. Holy [BLEEP]. We were right.
<v Aziz Ansari>她回复说，六分钟后，“是的，当然！”感叹号。“我同意。另外，下周四是我的生日。所以我希望下周末能开派对。”笑脸。该死。我们是对的。

00:42:04.980 --> 00:42:13.200
<v Aziz Ansari>Thank you, guys, so much for coming out. We really appreciate it.
<v Aziz Ansari>非常感谢大家的到来。我们真的很感激。

00:42:13.200 --> 00:42:48.450
<v Ira Glass>Aziz Ansari. The book that he wrote with Eric Klinenberg is called Modern Romance . Jonathan Menjivar is one of the producers of our show.
<v Ira Glass>阿兹·安萨里。他和埃里克·克林伯格写的书叫《现代浪漫》。乔纳森·曼吉瓦尔是我们节目的制作人之一。

00:42:48.450 --> 00:43:03.760
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, "On A Quiet Street in Richmond." So all through this hour today, we have documents that describe big dramatic things in the most "just the facts, ma'am" language possible, like a captain's log usually does. And we found this article from 1853 that's a good example of this.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，“里士满宁静的街道上”。所以今天在这一个小时里，我们有描述巨大戏剧性事件的文档，使用了尽可能“只是事实，女士”的语言，就像船长日志通常那样。我们发现了1853年的这篇文章，就是一个很好的例子。

00:43:03.760 --> 00:43:18.900
<v Ira Glass>In the article, a writer named William Chambers went out to witness and report on slave auctions. Apparently enough people had written about these in a very melodramatic and sensationalistic way that Chambers felt like somebody needed to go out and give a true, factual account.
<v Ira Glass>在文章中，一位名叫威廉·钱伯斯（William Chambers）的作家去观察和报道奴隶拍卖。显然，已经有足够多的人以非常戏剧化和耸人听闻的方式写过这些事情，钱伯斯觉得需要有人出去提供真实、客观的记录。

00:43:18.900 --> 00:43:31.560
<v John Ellison Conlee>Everything is described precisely as it occurred, without passion or prejudice. It would not have been difficult to be sentimental on a subject which appeals so strongly to the feelings but I preferred telling the simple truth.
<v John Ellison Conlee>一切都精确地描述了发生的事情，没有激情或偏见。在处理一个如此强烈地诉诸情感的主题时，表现得感伤并不困难，但我更喜欢说出简单的事实。

00:43:31.560 --> 00:43:42.850
<v Ira Glass>Our excerpts of Chambers' article were read for us by actor John Ellison Conlee. By 1853, slavery was controversial, and Chambers opposed it. He was a Scottish guy, an outsider.
<v Ira Glass>钱伯斯文章的节选是由演员约翰·埃里森·康利（John Ellison Conlee）为我们朗读的。到1853年，奴隶制是一个有争议的问题，钱伯斯反对它。他是个苏格兰人，局外人。

00:43:42.850 --> 00:43:59.690
<v Ira Glass>And so he spent a couple days going from one auction shop to another in Richmond, Virginia. Chambers explains that he's seen advertisements for slave sales. And when he went to the addresses, he found a narrow short street between two main streets in the city, brick houses on either side where there were a few small auction shops.
<v Ira Glass>所以他花了几周时间从里士满的一家拍卖行走到另一家。钱伯斯解释说他看过奴隶销售的广告。当他走到这些地址时，他发现两大街之间的狭窄街道，两边是砖房，那里有几家小型拍卖行。

00:43:59.690 --> 00:44:11.730
<v Ira Glass>On this particular day, it was pretty empty. Shops were identifiable by little red flags that hung outside their doors. And pinned to each flag was a piece of paper listing the men, women, and children who were going to be sold that day.
<v Ira Glass>在这一天，这里相当空旷。店铺可以通过门外悬挂的小红旗来识别。每一面旗子上都钉着一张纸，列出了那天要被卖掉的男人、女人和孩子。

00:44:11.730 --> 00:44:31.930
<v John Ellison Conlee>Conceive the idea of a large shop with two windows and a door between, no shelving or counters inside, the interior a spacious, dismal apartment, not well swept. The only furniture a desk at one of the windows and a bench at one side of the shop, three feet high with two steps to it from the floor.
<v John Ellison Conlee>设想一下一家大店，两个窗户，中间有一扇门，里面没有架子或柜台，内部是一个宽敞、阴郁的公寓，没有扫干净。唯一的家具是窗户边的一张桌子和店边上的一张长凳，高三英尺，有两级台阶从地面通向它。

00:44:31.930 --> 00:45:00.910
<v John Ellison Conlee>Conceive the idea of this dismal looking place with nobody in it but three Negro children who, as I entered, were playing at auctioneering each other. An intensely black little Negro of four or five years of age was standing on the bench-- or block, as it is called-- with an equally black girl, about a year younger, by his side whom he was pretending to sell by bids to another black child who was rolling about the floor.
<v John Ellison Conlee>设想一下这个阴郁的地方，除了三个黑人孩子外一个人也没有，我进去时，他们正在玩拍卖对方的游戏。一个四五岁、黑得发亮的黑人小男孩站在长凳——或者叫拍卖台——上，身边站着一个大约小一岁、同样黑的女孩，他假装要把她卖给另一个在地上滚来滚去的黑人孩子。

00:45:00.910 --> 00:45:23.490
<v John Ellison Conlee>My appearance did not interrupt the merriment. The little auctioneer continued his play and appeared to enjoy the joke of selling the girl who stood demurely by his side. "$50 for the girl. $50, $50. I sell this here fine girl for $50," was uttered, with appropriate gestures in imitation, doubtless, of the scenes he had seen and acted daily on the spot.
<v John Ellison Conlee>我的出现并没有打断欢乐。小拍卖官继续他的游戏，似乎很享受卖掉那个羞怯地站在他身边的女孩的笑话。“50美元买这个女孩。50美元，50美元。我把这个好女孩卖50美元，”他带着适当的手势模仿说，毫无疑问，模仿的是他每天在现场看到和演出的场景。

00:45:23.490 --> 00:45:39.580
<v John Ellison Conlee>I spoke a few words but was scarcely understood. And the fun went on as if I had not been present. So I left them, happy and rehearsing what was likely soon to be their own fate.
<v John Ellison Conlee>我说了几句话，但几乎没人听懂。游戏继续进行，仿佛我根本没在场一样。所以我离开了他们，快乐地排练着他们自己很快就会面临的命运。

00:45:39.580 --> 00:46:01.030
<v Ira Glass>At 9:30 AM, at another store, Chambers is approached by a salesman and asked if he's there to buy a slave. Chambers explains that he is not. He's simply there to witness the sales and gather information, which seems fine with the salesman. A lanky man sits down next to Chambers wearing what Chambers calls a wide awake hat, which is a hat with a kind of low round crown and a wide brim.
<v Ira Glass>上午9:30，在另一家店，一名推销员走近钱伯斯，问他是否是来买奴隶的。钱伯斯解释说他不是。他只是来见证销售并收集信息的，这在推销员看来没什么问题。一个瘦长的男人坐在钱伯斯旁边，戴着钱伯斯所谓的“清醒帽”，那是一种带有某种低圆顶和宽帽檐的帽子。

00:46:01.030 --> 00:46:25.070
<v John Ellison Conlee>Looking towards the door, I observe the subjects of sale. The man and boy indicated by the paper on the red flag enter together, whence, as the day was chilly, they edge themselves towards the fire in the corner where I was seated. I was now between the two parties, the white man on the right and the old and young Negro on the left. And I waited to see what would take place.
<v John Ellison Conlee>看向门外，我观察到销售对象。纸上和红旗上指出的男人和男孩一起进入，因为天气寒冷，他们向角落里的火炉边挤去。我现在处于双方之间，右边是白人，左边是老黑人和小黑人。我等着看会发生什么。

00:46:25.070 --> 00:46:38.950
<v John Ellison Conlee>The sight of the Negroes at once attracted the attention of Wide Awake. He kept keenly eyeing the pair as if to see what they were good for. Under this searched gaze, the man and boy were a little abashed but said nothing.
<v John Ellison Conlee>黑人的视线立刻吸引了“清醒”的注意。他一直敏锐地注视着这对父子，仿佛在看他们有什么用。在这种搜索的注视下，男人和男孩有点窘迫，但什么也没说。

00:46:38.950 --> 00:46:55.070
<v John Ellison Conlee>Their appearance had little of the repulsiveness we are apt to associate with the idea of slaves. They were dressed in a grey woolen coat, pants, and waistcoat, colored cotton neck cloths, clean shirts, coarse woolen stockings, and stout shoes.
<v John Ellison Conlee>他们的外表几乎没有我们习惯于与奴隶想法联系在一起的排斥感。他们穿着灰色的羊毛外套、裤子和背心，彩色的棉质颈巾，干净的衬衫，粗羊毛袜和结实的鞋子。

00:46:55.070 --> 00:47:15.910
<v John Ellison Conlee>Moved by a sudden impulse, Wide Awake left his seat and, rounding the back of my chair, began to grasp at the man's arms, as if to feel their muscular capacity. He then examined his hands and fingers, and last of all told him to open his mouth and show his teeth, which he did in a submissive manner.
<v John Ellison Conlee>被一种突然的冲动所驱使，“清醒”离开了座位，绕过我的椅背，开始抓住男人的手臂，仿佛要感受他们的肌肉能力。然后他检查了他的手和手指，最后告诉他张开嘴并展示他的牙齿，他以顺从的方式做了。

00:47:15.910 --> 00:47:30.920
<v John Ellison Conlee>Having finished these examinations, Wide Awake resumed his seat. I asked the elder Negro what was his age. He said he did not know. I next inquired how old the boy was. He said he was seven years of age.
<v John Ellison Conlee>检查完毕，“清醒”回到了座位。我问那个年长的黑人他的年龄。他说他不知道。我接着问男孩几岁了。他说他七岁。

00:47:30.920 --> 00:47:37.080
<v John Ellison Conlee>On asking the man if the boy was his son, he said he was not. He was his cousin.
<v John Ellison Conlee>当我问那个男人男孩是否是他的儿子时，他说不是。他是他的表弟。

00:47:37.080 --> 00:47:47.360
<v Ira Glass>Chambers and the man that he calls Wide Awake wait around for a while for the auction to start and then get tired of waiting. And they leave the shop to check out another one further up the street.
<v Ira Glass>钱伯斯和那个他称为“清醒”的人在那里等了一会儿拍卖开始，然后等得不耐烦了。他们离开了商店，去检查街道上方的另一家。

00:47:47.360 --> 00:48:10.310
<v John Ellison Conlee>Here, according to the announcement on the paper stuck to the flag, there were to be sold a woman and three children, a young woman, three men, a middle aged woman, and a little boy. Already a crowd had met. A few were seated near a fire on the right-hand side, and others stood around an iron stove in the middle of the apartment. The whole place had a dilapidated appearance.
<v John Ellison Conlee>这里，根据旗子上贴的纸上的公告，要卖的是一个女人和三个孩子，一个年轻女人，三个男人，一个中年女人和一个小男孩。人群已经聚集了。少数人坐在右侧火炉附近，其他人站在公寓中间的铁炉旁。整个地方看起来破败不堪。

00:48:10.310 --> 00:48:28.650
<v John Ellison Conlee>From a back window, there was a view into a ruinous courtyard, beyond which, in a hollow, accessible by a side lane, stood a shabby brick house on which the word jail was inscribed in large black letters on a white ground. I imagined it to be a depot for the reception of Negroes.
<v John Ellison Conlee>从后面的窗户，可以看到一个破败的院子，在那个院子下面，通过一条侧巷，矗立着一栋破旧的砖房，上面用大的黑色字母在白色的地面上刻着“监狱”这个词。我以为这是接收黑人的仓库。

00:48:28.650 --> 00:48:45.030
<v John Ellison Conlee>On my arrival, and while making these preliminary observations, the lots for sale had not made their appearance. In about five minutes afterwards, they were ushered in, one after the other, under the charge of a mulatto, who seemed to act as a principal assistant.
<v John Ellison Conlee>当我到达时，在做这些初步观察的同时，要出售的货物还没有出现。大约五分钟后，在一个看起来像主要助手的混血儿的带领下，他们被一个接一个地迎了进来。

00:48:45.030 --> 00:49:08.890
<v John Ellison Conlee>I saw no whips, chains, or any other engine of force, nor did such appear to be required. All the lots took their seats on two long forms near the stove. None showed any signs of resistance, nor did anyone utter a word. Their manner was that of perfect humility and resignation.
<v John Ellison Conlee>我没有看到鞭子、锁链或任何其他武力引擎，似乎也不需要。所有的货物都坐在火炉附近的两个长凳上。没有一个人表现出任何抵抗的迹象，也没有一个人说一句话。他们的态度是完美的谦逊和顺从。

00:49:08.890 --> 00:49:29.680
<v John Ellison Conlee>As soon as all were seated, there was a general examination of their respective merits by feeling their arms, looking into their mouths. Yet there was no abrupt rudeness in making these examinations, no coarse or domineering language was employed. The three Negro men were dressed in the usual manner, in grey woolen clothing.
<v John Ellison Conlee>一旦所有人都坐下，便开始了通过触摸手臂、看向嘴里来普遍检查他们各自的优点。但这些检查中没有突兀的粗鲁，也没有使用粗鲁或专横的语言。三个黑人男人穿着通常的方式，穿着灰色羊毛衣服。

00:49:29.680 --> 00:49:50.620
<v John Ellison Conlee>The woman with three children excited my peculiar attention. She was neatly attired with a colored handkerchief bound around her head and wore a white apron over her gown. Her children were all girls, one of them a baby at the breast three months old, and the others two and three years of age, respectively, rigged out with clean white pinafores.
<v John Ellison Conlee>那个带着三个孩子的女人引起了我特别的关注。她穿着整齐，头上扎着彩色手帕，长袍外面系着白色围裙。她的孩子都是女孩，其中一个是三个月大的吃奶婴儿，另外两个分别是两岁和三岁，穿着干净的白色围兜。

00:49:50.620 --> 00:50:10.110
<v John Ellison Conlee>There was not a tear or an emotion visible in the whole party. Everything seemed to be considered as a matter of course. And the change of owners was possibly looked forward to do with as much indifference as ordinary hired servants anticipate a removal from one employer to another.
<v John Ellison Conlee>整个队伍里没有一丝眼泪或情绪。一切似乎都被认为是理所当然的。所有者变更可能被视为像普通雇佣仆人预期从一个雇主换到另一个雇主一样无所谓。

00:50:10.110 --> 00:50:21.610
<v John Ellison Conlee>While intending purchasers were proceeding with personal examinations of the several lots, I took the liberty of putting a few questions to the mother of the children. The following was our conversation.
<v John Ellison Conlee>当潜在买家在进行对各种货物的个人检查时，我冒昧地问了孩子们母亲几个问题。以下是我们的对话。

00:50:21.610 --> 00:50:32.330
<v John Ellison Conlee>Are you a married woman? Yes, sir. How many children have you had? Seven. Where is your husband? In Madison County.
<v John Ellison Conlee>你是已婚妇女吗？是的，先生。你有几个孩子？七个。你丈夫在哪里？在麦迪逊县。

00:50:32.330 --> 00:50:45.140
<v John Ellison Conlee>When did you part from him? On Wednesday, two days ago. Where you sorry to part from him? Yes, sir, she replied with a deep sigh. My heart was almost broke.
<v John Ellison Conlee>你什么时候离开他的？周三，两天前。你很难过离开他吗？是的，先生，她深深地叹了口气回答道。我的心都要碎了。

00:50:45.140 --> 00:50:54.520
<v John Ellison Conlee>Why is your master selling you? I don't know. He wants money to buy some land. Suppose he sells me for that.
<v John Ellison Conlee>你主人为什么要卖你？我不知道。他想要钱去买些土地。假设他为了那个卖了我。

00:50:54.520 --> 00:51:17.510
<v John Ellison Conlee>There might not be a word of truth in these answers, for I had no means of testing their correctness. But the woman seemed to speak unreservedly. And I am inclined to think that she said nothing but what, if necessary, could be substantiated.
<v John Ellison Conlee>这些回答可能一点真实成分都没有，因为我没有办法测试它们的准确性。但这个女人似乎毫无保留地说话。我倾向于认为除了在必要时能被证实的东西外，她什么也没说。

00:51:17.510 --> 00:51:32.080
<v Ira Glass>When the woman and her three children are put up on the auction block, bidding starts at $850. But it only gets up to $890. That won't do, gentleman, the auctioneer says. I cannot take such a low price. And the women and her children step down from the block.
<v Ira Glass>当那个女人和她的三个孩子被放在拍卖台上时，竞标从850美元开始。但它只上升到890美元。那不行，先生们，拍卖商说。我不能接受这么低的价格。那个女人和她的孩子从台上走下来。

00:51:32.080 --> 00:51:43.130
<v Ira Glass>$890 is the equivalent of nearly $26,000 today. Chambers reprints a price list. That's what one adult would sell for, not an adult and three kids.
<v Ira Glass>890美元相当于今天的近26,000美元。钱伯斯重印了一份价格表。那是卖掉一个成年人的价格，不是一个成年人和三个孩子。

00:51:43.130 --> 00:51:51.840
<v Ira Glass>The most interesting passage in Chambers' report is where he speculates about what the slaves feel about all this. Then in this passage he starts fine.
<v Ira Glass>钱伯斯报告中最有趣的一段是他推测奴隶们对此有什么感觉。然后在这段中他开始做得不错。

00:51:51.840 --> 00:52:06.850
<v John Ellison Conlee>There was an entire absence of emotion in the looks of men, women, and children thus seated, preparatory to being sold. This does not correspond with the ordinary accounts of slave sales which are represented as tearful and harrowing.
<v John Ellison Conlee>在那些坐在那里准备被出售的男人、女人的脸上完全没有情绪。这与通常描述的代表泪流满面和令人心碎的奴隶销售的描述不符。

00:52:06.850 --> 00:52:12.250
<v Ira Glass>And then, Chambers takes a turn. He says something that is really hard to imagine somebody today writing.
<v Ira Glass>然后，钱伯斯转了一个弯。他说了一些真的很难想象今天的人会写的东西。

00:52:12.250 --> 00:52:28.850
<v John Ellison Conlee>My belief is that none of the parties felt deeply on the subject, or at least that any distress they experienced was but momentary, soon passed away and was forgotten. One of my reasons for this opinion rests on a trifling incident which occurred.
<v John Ellison Conlee>我的信念是，没有人对这个主题有深刻的感受，或者至少他们所经历的任何痛苦都只是片刻的，很快就过去了并被遗忘了。我持此观点的理由之一是发生的一个琐碎事件。

00:52:28.850 --> 00:52:50.190
<v John Ellison Conlee>While waiting for the commencement of the sale, one of the gentleman present amused himself with a pointer dog which, at command, stood on its hind legs and took pieces of bread from his pocket. These tricks greatly entertained the row of Negroes, old and young. And the poor woman, whose heart three minutes before was almost broken, now laughed as heartily as anyone.
<v John Ellison Conlee>在等待拍卖开始时，在场的一位绅士用一只指示犬逗乐自己，那只狗在命令下用后腿站立，从他口袋里拿面包片。这些把戏极大地娱乐了那些老老少少的黑人队伍。那个三分钟前心几乎碎掉的可怜女人，现在笑得和任何人一样大声。

00:52:50.190 --> 00:53:07.450
<v Ira Glass>Today, this just seems wrong-headed. Of course you could be deeply affected by something you're going through and still, you know, laugh at a dog doing tricks. Of course, any of us could contain both those things at the same time. It's weird that Chambers doesn't see that.
<v Ira Glass>今天，这看起来只是思路错误。当然，你可能对你正在经历的事情深深影响，同时，你知道，笑着看狗表演把戏。当然，我们任何人都可能同时包含这两样东西。钱伯斯没看到这一点很奇怪。

00:53:07.450 --> 00:53:19.600
<v Ira Glass>A scholar who's written about Chambers told me that Chambers was actually not a keen observer of people in his writing. He doesn't do much character observation when he writes. So it's possible he just was not super perceptive about people's feelings.
<v Ira Glass>一位写过钱伯斯的学者告诉我，钱伯斯在写作中实际上不是一个敏锐的观察者。当他写作时，他没有做太多性格观察。所以很可能他只是对人的感受不是很敏锐。

00:53:19.600 --> 00:53:45.910
<v Ira Glass>But also, when you read more of Chambers, it's clear he thinks that white people are superior to black people. He wrote, quote, "A black man is only a kind of man. He stands upright on two legs, his hands to work, wears clothes, can cook his food, a point not reached by monkeys," Chambers writes. "Perhaps," Chambers says, "there's something wrong with his craniological development," which, I guess he means, not as smart.
<v Ira Glass>但同样，当你读更多钱伯斯的著作时，很清楚他认为白人比黑人优越。他写道，引用，“黑人只是一种人。他双腿直立，手工作，穿衣服，能煮食物，这是猴子未达到的点，”钱伯斯写道。“也许，”钱伯斯说，“他的颅骨发育有问题，”我想他的意思是不那么聪明。

00:53:45.910 --> 00:54:19.120
<v Ira Glass>So he went on a mission to document the reality of slavery for his readers. He was that enlightened. He was that against it. But that was as far as he went. On his visit to Richmond, he was not able to see the people in front of him for what they really were, which was, of course, exactly the same as him.
<v Ira Glass>所以他带着记录奴隶制现实的任务为他的读者而去。他就是那么开明。他就是那么反对它。但那就是他走的全部路程。在他访问里士满时，他没能看到他面前的人真正是什么，当然，和他是完全一样的。

00:54:19.120 --> 00:54:36.740
<v Ira Glass>Act Four, "A Brief History of Us." So to close today's show, we asked a fiction writer, Etgar Keret, to create a short story that works like a captain's log that's told completely through a series of unemotional facts. Here's what he wrote. It's read for us by actress Sue Scott.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕，“我们简史”。为了结束今天的节目，我们要求一位小说家埃特加·克雷特（Etgar Keret），创作一个像船长日志一样工作的短篇故事，完全通过一系列无情感的事实来叙述。这是他写的。由女演员苏·斯科特（Sue Scott）为我们朗读。

00:54:36.740 --> 00:54:55.670
<v Sue Scott>At first, we were a cell, then an amoeba, than a fish. And after a very long and frustrating era, we became a lizard. That was the era when, as we recall, the earth felt soft and unsteady beneath our feet. So we climbed a tree.
<v Sue Scott>起初，我们是一个细胞，然后是一个变形虫，然后是一条鱼。在一个非常长且令人沮丧的时代之后，我们成了一只蜥蜴。那是我们记得地球在脚下感觉柔软和不稳定的时代。所以我们爬上了一棵树。

00:54:55.670 --> 00:55:11.090
<v Sue Scott>Up there in the treetops, we felt secure. At some point, we climbed down and started walking upright and speaking. And as soon as we began speaking, we just couldn't stop.
<v Sue Scott>在树顶上，我们感到安全。在某个时候，我们爬了下来，开始直立行走并说话。一旦我们开始说话，我们就再也停不下来了。

00:55:11.090 --> 00:55:25.950
<v Sue Scott>After that, we watched a lot of TV. It was a fantastic era. We always laughed in the wrong places. And people stared and said, what's so funny? And we didn't even bother answering. That's how little we cared.
<v Sue Scott>在那之后，我们看了很多电视。那是一个奇妙的时代。我们总是在错误的地方大笑。人们盯着看并说，有什么好笑的？我们甚至懒得回答。我们就是这样一点也不在乎。

00:55:25.950 --> 00:55:45.230
<v Sue Scott>We promised ourselves we'd find a job we loved. And when that didn't work out, we settled for a job we didn't hate. And we felt lucky, and then unlucky, and then lucky again.
<v Sue Scott>我们答应自己会找到一份我们热爱的工作。当那没成功时，我们满足于一份我们不讨厌的工作。我们感到幸运，然后倒霉，然后又幸运了。

00:55:45.230 --> 00:56:11.680
<v Sue Scott>And suddenly, our parents were dying. Then they died. A second before they departed, we held their hand really tight and told him we forgave them for everything-- everything. And our voice broke when we said that, because we weren't convinced we were telling the truth. And we were afraid they could sense it.
<v Sue Scott>突然，我们的父母要死了。然后他们死了。在他们离去前一秒，我们紧紧抓住他们的手，告诉他们我们原谅了他们所做的一切——一切。当我们说那句话时，我们的声音颤抖了，因为我们不相信我们说的是真话。我们害怕他们能感觉到。

00:56:11.680 --> 00:56:36.000
<v Sue Scott>Less than a year after that, our son was born. And he also climbed a tree and felt secure up there. And he came down at some point, too, and went off to college. Then we were left alone, and it started getting cold, not like the other time eons ago when we hid in boroughs and peered out while the dinosaurs froze to death, but still depressingly cold.
<v Sue Scott>那之后不到一年，我们的儿子出生了。他也爬上了一棵树，并在上面感到安全。他也爬了下来，去上大学了。然后我们被留下来孤身一人，天气开始变冷，不像几亿年前我们躲在洞穴里偷窥恐龙冷死时的那样，但仍然令人沮丧地冷。

00:56:36.000 --> 00:56:50.460
<v Sue Scott>So we went to a drama workshop, because our friend said it would do us good. They gave us a series of improv exercises. And in the first one, we poisoned each other. And in the second one, we cheated on each other.
<v Sue Scott>所以我们去了一个戏剧工作室，因为我们的朋友说这会对我们有好处。他们给我们一系列即兴练习。在第一个练习中，我们互相毒害。在第二个中，我们互相欺骗。

00:56:50.460 --> 00:57:03.330
<v Sue Scott>And in the third one, the instructor, who spoke English with a heavy indistinct accent said, now switch partners. And within seconds, it wasn't us anymore.
<v Sue Scott>在第三个中，那个用沉重、模糊口音说英语的教练说，现在交换伙伴。在几秒钟内，这就不是我们了。

00:57:03.330 --> 00:57:24.090
<v Sue Scott>The new man who was my partner said, let's do a sketch where you're a baby, and I give birth to you and nurse you and protect you from all evil. And I said, sure, why not? But by the time he'd finished giving birth to me and nursing me and protecting me from all evil, our time was up.
<v Sue Scott>我的伙伴新男人说，让我们演一个素描，你是一个婴儿，我生下你，哺育你，保护你免受一切邪恶。我说，当然，为什么不？但在他生下我、哺育我并保护我免受一切邪恶之后，我们的时间到了。

00:57:24.090 --> 00:57:43.820
<v Sue Scott>And the instructor with a strange accent asked if the exercise had made any primal memory surface. And I said it hadn't, because I didn't want to admit that it had brought back warm primordial memories from millions of years ago, from before we even emerged from the water.
<v Sue Scott>带有奇怪口音的教练问练习是否让任何原始记忆浮现。我说没有，因为我不想承认它带回了数百万年前，在我们从水中浮出之前那些温暖的原始记忆。

00:57:43.820 --> 00:58:04.350
<v Sue Scott>Afterwards at home, we got into an argument over something really dumb and had the biggest fight we'd ever had since we were created. We yelled and cried and broke things, the kind of things that if you'd asked us a day earlier we'd have told you were unbreakable.
<v Sue Scott>之后在家里，我们因为一些非常愚蠢的事情争吵，并进行了我们自诞生以来最大的一场架。我们大喊大叫，哭了并打碎了东西，如果你早一天问我们，我们肯定会告诉你这些东西是不可打碎的那种。

00:58:04.350 --> 00:58:22.210
<v Sue Scott>Then we packed our stuff up in a suitcase and shoved whatever didn't fit in the suitcase into plastic grocery bags. And we dragged all that behind us like homeless people to the apartment where a very wealthy friend of ours lived. And he put a sheet out on his plush sofa for us.
<v Sue Scott>然后我们将东西打包在行李箱里，把塞不进行李箱的东西塞进塑料杂货袋里。我们像流浪汉一样把所有东西拖到我们一位非常富有的朋友居住的公寓里。他在他豪华的沙发上为我们铺了一张床单。

00:58:22.210 --> 00:58:42.320
<v Sue Scott>The friend told us that it might seem like the end of the world now. But by morning, all the rage and hurt feelings would melt away, and everything would look different. And we said, no, something had been broken. Something had been torn apart, something we would never be able to mend or forgive.
<v Sue Scott>朋友告诉我们现在世界末日看起来可能到了。但到了早上，所有的愤怒和受伤的感情都会融化，一切看起来都会不一样。我们说，不，有什么东西被打破了。有什么东西被撕裂了，我们永远无法修复或原谅的东西。

00:58:42.320 --> 00:59:14.860
<v Sue Scott>The friend lit an imported Slims cigarette and said, OK, maybe so. But can I just ask, why do you keep talking in the plural? Instead of answering, I just looked around and realized I was alone-- truly, completely alone.
<v Sue Scott>朋友点燃了一支进口Slims香烟说，好的，也许是这样。但我能问一下吗，为什么你们一直用复数谈话？没有回答，我只是环顾四周，意识到我是一个人——真正地、彻底地一个人。

00:59:14.860 --> 01:00:01.410
<v Ira Glass>Sue Scott, reading a story by Etgar Keret. Etgar's latest book is a memoir called The Seven Good Years . His story was translated from Hebrew by Jessica [INAUDIBLE].
<v Ira Glass>苏·斯科特，朗读埃特加·克雷特的故事。埃特加的最新书是一本叫《七个好年头》（The Seven Good Years）的回忆录。他的故事由杰西卡翻译自希伯来语。

01:00:01.410 --> 01:00:09.240
<v Ira Glass>Our program was produced today by Zoe Chace and myself with Sean Cole, Stephanie Foo, Chana Joffe-Walt, Miki Meek, Jonathan Menjivar, Brian Reed, Robyn Semien, Alissa Shipp, and Nancy Updike.
<v Ira Glass>我们的节目今天由佐伊·蔡斯（Zoe Chace）和我自己制作，还有肖恩·科尔（Sean Cole）、斯蒂芬妮·富（Stephanie Foo）、查纳·乔夫-沃尔特（Chana Joffe-Walt）、米基·米克（Miki Meek）、乔纳森·曼吉瓦尔、布莱恩·里德（Brian Reed）、罗宾·塞门（Robyn Semien）、艾丽莎·希普（Alissa Shipp）和南希·厄普代克（Nancy Updike）。

01:00:09.240 --> 01:00:19.250
<v Ira Glass>Editing help from Joel Lovell. Production help from Simon Adler, Seth Lind, our operations director, Emily Condon, who's our production manager. Elise Bergerson's our office manager. Elna Baker scouts stories for our show.
<v Ira Glass>乔尔·洛弗尔（Joel Lovell）的编辑帮助。西蒙·阿德勒（Simon Adler）、塞思·林德（Seth Lind）的制作帮助，我们的运营总监艾米丽·康登（Emily Condon）是我们的制作经理。埃莉斯·伯格森（Elise Bergerson）是我们的办公室经理。埃尔娜·贝克（Elna Baker）为我们的节目搜寻故事。

01:00:19.250 --> 01:00:24.140
<v Ira Glass>Research help today from Michelle Harris and Christopher Swetala. Music help from Damien Graef and Rob Geddis.
<v Ira Glass>今天来自米歇尔·哈里斯（Michelle Harris）和克里斯托弗·斯韦塔拉（Christopher Swetala）的研究帮助。来自达米安·格拉夫（Damien Graef）和罗布·格迪斯（Rob Geddis）的音乐帮助。

01:00:24.140 --> 01:00:43.410
<v Ira Glass>[ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS]
<v Ira Glass>[感谢词]

01:00:43.410 --> 01:01:01.350
<v Ira Glass>Our website, thisamericanlife.org. This American Life is delivered to public radio stations by PRX, the Public Radio Exchange. Thanks, as always, to our program's co-founder, Mr. Torey Malatia. You know, he donated blood this week. And when it was all over, I don't know. He told me he was really feeling out of sorts.
<v Ira Glass>我们的网站，thisamericanlife.org。《美国生活》由PRX，公共广播交易所交付给公共广播电台。像往常一样，感谢我们节目的联合创始人托里·马拉蒂亚（Torey Malatia）先生。你知道，他这周捐了血。当一切结束后，我不知道。他告诉我他真的感到不舒服。

01:01:01.350 --> 01:01:06.640
<v Dale>I said, "What's the status on the cookies? Yarr. Me so hungry."
<v Dale>我说，“饼干的情况怎么样了？呀。我好饿。”

01:01:06.640 --> 01:01:11.640
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass. Back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯。下周带着更多《美国生活》的故事回来。
